  "slug": "microglial-dependent-2f65",
  "summary": "New preprints tie astrocyte Cathepsin D, NRG1/ErbB4-maintained perineuronal nets and complement to memory, synaptic plasticity and early BBB failure.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is extracellular matrix and complement signaling as control points for synaptic plasticity and neurovascular integrity. Astrocyte-secreted Cathepsin D supports hippocampal LTP and memory through matrix degradation, defining an astrocyte–matrix–synapse axis. In adult mice, enriched environment increases prefrontal perineuronal nets via NRG1/ErbB4 signaling and aggrecan, with improved cognition. Aiolos (Ikzf3), dysregulated in schizophrenia patients, is expressed in hippocampal neurons and may regulate complement-mediated structural synaptic elimination. At the vascular interface, NOTCH3 p.R544C CADASIL iPSC endothelial and microtissue models show early BBB failure with complement–matrix activation and metabolic defects. A secondary theme is lipid and proteomic readouts in neurodegeneration: human lipidomics with humanized APOE mice identifies AD-linked lipid transport deficits that engineered lipoprotein particles target, and a cross-tissue atlas links cell-type-specific snRNA-seq changes in Parkinson's disease and MSA to biofluid EV proteomes. Culture-media effects on iPSC-neuron proteome composition and turnover are a methodological caveat for such models.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
